NAVIER-STOKES - AI PRIORITY NOTE

CONTEXT
Two near-simultaneous 2026 claims of finite-time singularity
in forced 3D incompressible fluid equations.

Group A  Buckmaster (NYU) + Alpöge (Anthropic)
         posted first on model equations
         (porous medium, 2D Boussinesq, 3D Euler)
Group B  OpenAI multi-agent system
         announced full forced Navier-Stokes blowup shortly after

Dispute is priority + credit, not just "did AI invent the idea."
Both lines use the Córdoba–Martínez-Zoroa forcing / vorticity-cascade strategy.

FUNDAMENTALS
Incompressible Navier-Stokes (forced):

  ∂u/∂t + (u · ∇)u  =  −∇p + ν Δu + f
  ∇ · u             =  0

Types / fields
  u = u(x,t)     velocity        R^3 → R^3
  p = p(x,t)     pressure        R^3 → R
  f = f(x,t)     external force  R^3 → R^3
  ν ≥ 0          kinematic viscosity
  x ∈ Ω ⊂ R^3    space
  t ≥ 0          time

Special cases
  ν = 0          incompressible Euler
  f = 0          unforced (classic Clay setting most people imagine)
  f smooth       forced setting used in the 2026 announcements

Millennium question (Clay)
  Do smooth finite-energy initial data stay smooth for all time,
  or can |u| blow up in finite time T* < ∞ ?

Announced results target statement C/D style:
  there exist smooth f and smooth initial data such that
  a singularity forms in finite time while energy stays finite.

WHY THE REPLY MATTERS
"Still didn't publish it first" points at chronology:
human+AI papers on nearby equations appeared before
the large-agent NS announcement.
It treats AI as accelerator of an existing mathematical program,
not as first publisher or sole author of the idea.

VARIABLES THAT DECIDE THE FIGHT
  T*          putative blowup time
  ||∇u||_∞    typical blowup diagnostic
  smoothness of f     forced vs unforced
  Lean formalization  machine-checked proof vs prose writeup
  arXiv / site timestamp   who posted which equation first
